WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.101
Dennis turns 65 in March.

00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:06.723
Medicare is the federal health program for people 65 and older,

00:00:06.723 --> 00:00:11.345
people under 65 who have received Social Security disability benefits for

00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:15.547
24 months, and people with end-stage renal disease or ALS.

00:00:15.547 --> 00:00:20.169
It is administered by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services.

00:00:20.169 --> 00:00:24.791
Part A, hospital insurance, is financed by the payroll tax and

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:28.993
is premium-free for people with 40 quarters of covered work.

00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:33.616
Part B, medical insurance, is financed by premiums and general revenue.

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.977
Parts A and B together are Original Medicare.

00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:40.339
Part C is Medicare Advantage and Part D

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:43.280
is drug coverage, covered in Lesson 43.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.317
Dennis’s initial enrollment period is seven months: the three months before

00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:51.355
his birthday month, the month itself and the three months after.

00:00:51.355 --> 00:00:56.126
If he already receives Social Security he is enrolled automatically; otherwise he applies.

00:00:56.126 --> 00:01:00.897
If he misses it, the general enrollment period runs January 1 to March

00:01:00.897 --> 00:01:05.302
31 each year, with coverage starting the month after he signs up.

00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:09.706
If he keeps working with employer coverage from an employer of 20

00:01:09.706 --> 00:01:14.110
or more, he may delay Part B without penalty and use an

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.781
eight-month special enrollment period after the employment or coverage ends.

00:01:17.781 --> 00:01:21.084
Late Part B enrollment carries a permanent penalty of

00:01:21.084 --> 00:01:24.020
10 percent for each full year of delay.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.604
Part A covers inpatient hospital care, skilled nursing facility care after a qualifying three-day

00:01:29.604 --> 00:01:34.788
inpatient stay, home health care, hospice and blood after the first three pints.

00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:40.372
Costs are organized by benefit period, which begins at admission and ends when the

00:01:40.372 --> 00:01:45.955
patient has been out of the hospital or skilled facility for 60 consecutive days.

00:01:45.955 --> 00:01:49.944
Each benefit period carries a deductible, 1,736 dollars in 2026.

00:01:49.944 --> 00:01:53.932
Days one through sixty are then covered in full; days

00:01:53.932 --> 00:01:57.920
61 through 90 require a daily coinsurance; and 60 lifetime

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.908
reserve days with a higher coinsurance can be used once.

00:02:01.908 --> 00:02:06.694
Skilled nursing care is covered in full for days one through twenty,

00:02:06.694 --> 00:02:11.480
with coinsurance for days 21 through 100 and nothing after day 100.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.990
Part B covers physician services, outpatient hospital care, durable medical equipment,

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:22.501
laboratory tests, ambulance, many preventive services without cost sharing, and a

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:27.010
limited set of drugs administered in a medical setting.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.017
Everyone pays a monthly premium, 202.90 dollars in

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:35.025
2026 for most people, more at higher incomes.

00:02:35.025 --> 00:02:41.537
After an annual deductible, 283 dollars in 2026, Medicare pays 80 percent of

00:02:41.537 --> 00:02:48.050
the approved amount and the beneficiary pays 20 percent with no out-of-pocket maximum.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:53.560
Part B does not cover routine dental, vision or hearing aids,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.070
custodial care, cosmetic surgery or most care outside the United States.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:04.080
These gaps are why Medicare supplement and Advantage plans exist.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.178
When Dennis sees a doctor, the approved amount

00:03:07.178 --> 00:03:09.889
is what Medicare allows for the service.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:15.311
A provider who accepts assignment agrees to take the approved amount as full payment,

00:03:15.311 --> 00:03:20.346
bills Medicare directly and collects only the deductible and 20 percent from Dennis.

00:03:20.346 --> 00:03:23.444
A participating provider accepts assignment for all patients.

00:03:23.444 --> 00:03:28.479
A nonparticipating provider may refuse assignment and charge up to 15 percent above

00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:33.513
the approved amount, the limiting charge, which Dennis pays as an excess charge.

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:37.773
Providers who opt out of Medicare entirely bill privately under contract.

00:03:37.773 --> 00:03:43.195
Dennis receives a Medicare Summary Notice showing what was billed, approved and paid, and

00:03:43.195 --> 00:03:48.230
an advance beneficiary notice warns him before a service Medicare may not cover.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:53.212
Dennis’s plan: enroll in Part A during his seven-month window because it is free.

00:03:53.212 --> 00:03:56.771
Since his employer has 60 employees and his group plan

00:03:56.771 --> 00:04:00.330
is primary, he may delay Part B without penalty and

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.889
enroll during the eight-month special enrollment period when he retires.

00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:07.448
When he does, his six-month Medicare supplement open enrollment begins.

00:04:07.448 --> 00:04:11.718
A hospital stay under Original Medicare costs him the Part A deductible;

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:15.989
a surgeon’s bill costs him 20 percent of the approved amount plus

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:19.548
any excess charge if the surgeon does not accept assignment.

00:04:19.548 --> 00:04:24.530
Those gaps, and the absence of any out-of-pocket maximum, drive the next three lessons.
